WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.970
Imagine asking an AI to design a quiet reading corner.

2
00:00:04.395 --> 00:00:08.712
The sofa, bookshelf, and floor lamp are all there. But when you sit down,

3
00:00:08.957 --> 00:00:12.217
the light is too far away, and reaching a book is awkward.

4
00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:16.354
All the objects are present. But is the design really complete?

5
00:00:17.907 --> 00:00:21.631
A good space should be accessible, its furniture should be usable,

6
00:00:21.966 --> 00:00:23.706
and the pieces should work together.

7
00:00:24.118 --> 00:00:28.770
Avoiding collisions and boundary violations is only the foundation. The

8
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:31.270
relationship between the seat, the lighting,

9
00:00:31.412 --> 00:00:33.628
and the bookshelf is what makes reading work.

10
00:00:35.157 --> 00:00:39.641
We define design intelligence as the ability to both design a scene and critique

11
00:00:39.654 --> 00:00:40.427
its quality.

12
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.855
Scene design concerns constructing physically valid,

13
00:00:44.139 --> 00:00:46.935
functionally correct, and layout-coherent scenes,

14
00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:51.420
while efficiently correcting errors without unnecessary trial and error.

15
00:00:51.832 --> 00:00:56.703
Scene critique assesses scene quality along the same dimensions and distinguishes

16
00:00:56.716 --> 00:00:58.675
better designs from poorer ones.

17
00:01:00.253 --> 00:01:04.261
Both capabilities remain challenging for general-purpose models.

18
00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:10.124
Our analysis of one hundred scenes generated by GPT-5.2 reveals inefficient

19
00:01:10.137 --> 00:01:15.202
correction caused by unnecessary trial and error. The model can identify many

20
00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:19.918
design errors, yet makes unnecessary corrective attempts before reaching an

21
00:01:19.931 --> 00:01:21.129
effective solution.

22
00:01:21.555 --> 00:01:28.049
The second challenge is insufficient scene critique ability. GPT-5.2 can favor a

23
00:01:28.062 --> 00:01:32.508
more open central space while overlooking functional organization and layout

24
00:01:32.521 --> 00:01:33.346
coherence.

25
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.940
Looking only at a finished scene is like seeing only the answer to a problem.

26
00:01:39.353 --> 00:01:41.994
You cannot see why the designer made those choices,

27
00:01:42.304 --> 00:01:45.229
what went wrong along the way, or how it was corrected.

28
00:01:45.641 --> 00:01:50.165
So we want the model to learn both the experience of designing a scene and the

29
00:01:50.177 --> 00:01:52.420
experience of critiquing its quality.

30
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:59.841
Scene-OTA-35K provides design experience from one thousand scenes and thirty-five

31
00:01:59.854 --> 00:02:02.921
thousand Observation, Thought, and Action steps.

32
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:05.549
But we do not simply keep every attempt.

33
00:02:05.962 --> 00:02:09.222
Raw trajectories contain unnecessary trial and error,

34
00:02:09.776 --> 00:02:14.196
reflecting the exploration process rather than effective design strategies.

35
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.611
Causal Trajectory Consolidation begins with error-aware candidate identification,

36
00:02:21.165 --> 00:02:25.818
using changes in scene state and remaining errors to flag potentially ineffective

37
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.771
operations.

38
00:02:27.184 --> 00:02:32.093
Correction-effect evaluation then assesses contributions to effective correction

39
00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:37.635
or necessary intermediate states. Only ineffective operations without downstream

40
00:02:37.647 --> 00:02:39.400
contributions are removed.

41
00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:44.542
We then replay the trajectory to verify the same final scene. The average

42
00:02:44.555 --> 00:02:49.361
trajectory becomes shorter, from about one hundred and ten steps to about thirty.

43
00:02:50.949 --> 00:02:56.580
Scene-OTC-15K provides scene critique experience. Starting from high-quality

44
00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:59.892
scenes, we introduce mild layout deviations,

45
00:03:00.188 --> 00:03:03.693
functional degradation, and severe physical violations.

46
00:03:04.119 --> 00:03:07.147
The model sees how the same room becomes less usable.

47
00:03:07.572 --> 00:03:11.490
About fifteen thousand comparison examples provide Observation,

48
00:03:11.773 --> 00:03:13.526
Thought, and Critic supervision.

49
00:03:15.086 --> 00:03:19.635
Applying perturbations of different strengths to the same object allows automatic

50
00:03:19.648 --> 00:03:21.981
ranking based on perturbation severity.

51
00:03:22.406 --> 00:03:26.994
Applying the same perturbation to different objects requires manual annotation,

52
00:03:27.303 --> 00:03:31.040
because its impact depends on the affected object's functional role.

53
00:03:31.465 --> 00:03:33.630
The goal is to learn design principles,

54
00:03:33.824 --> 00:03:36.633
beyond simply preferring one image over another.

55
00:03:38.157 --> 00:03:42.873
Joint supervised fine-tuning on Scene-OTA and Scene-OTC teaches the model to

56
00:03:42.886 --> 00:03:46.933
construct physically valid scenes, imitate efficient corrections,

57
00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.879
and evaluate scene quality.

58
00:03:49.291 --> 00:03:53.389
This gives us Scene-Builder, a model with an initial foundation of design

59
00:03:53.402 --> 00:03:54.342
experience.

60
00:03:54.755 --> 00:03:58.221
The next question is: when several edits are possible,

61
00:03:58.505 --> 00:04:00.348
can it learn to make a better choice?

62
00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.327
We let the model explore multiple candidate actions from the same parent scene.

63
00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:09.922
A frozen Critic compares the resulting child scenes.

64
00:04:10.335 --> 00:04:15.038
It judges both which candidate is better and whether each edit actually improves

65
00:04:15.051 --> 00:04:16.056
on the parent scene.

66
00:04:17.615 --> 00:04:22.551
The branch-ranking reward compares sibling scenes. The improvement reward compares

67
00:04:22.577 --> 00:04:24.458
each child scene with its parent.

68
00:04:24.883 --> 00:04:27.525
The format reward supports valid tool calls,

69
00:04:28.002 --> 00:04:32.074
while the global length and object-completeness rewards encourage efficient yet

70
00:04:32.087 --> 00:04:34.561
complete scene construction. Together,

71
00:04:34.626 --> 00:04:39.110
these rewards guide better design decisions and reduce unnecessary trial and

72
00:04:39.123 --> 00:04:39.535
error.

73
00:04:39.948 --> 00:04:42.177
Through this reinforcement learning stage,

74
00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:44.342
Scene-Builder becomes Scene-Designer.

75
00:04:45.907 --> 00:04:50.559
Scene-Designer reduces unnecessary trial and error by learning more direct and

76
00:04:50.572 --> 00:04:52.505
effective correction strategies.

77
00:04:52.917 --> 00:04:57.763
For interaction orientation errors, the share of cases requiring five or more

78
00:04:57.775 --> 00:05:02.054
corrective moves falls from forty percent with Scene-Builder to seven percent with

79
00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:02.917
Scene-Designer.

80
00:05:03.342 --> 00:05:08.265
For window accessibility blockage, the share falls from twenty-one percent to five

81
00:05:08.278 --> 00:05:08.935
percent.

82
00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.892
In fifty-five test groups, each containing four scenes,

83
00:05:14.214 --> 00:05:16.586
the model must get the entire ranking right.

84
00:05:17.011 --> 00:05:22.861
GPT-5.2 achieves fifteen percent accuracy. A model trained only on critique

85
00:05:22.874 --> 00:05:25.245
experience reaches fifty-one percent.

86
00:05:25.671 --> 00:05:28.209
With joint training on scene design and critique,

87
00:05:28.544 --> 00:05:30.915
accuracy reaches seventy-six percent.

88
00:05:32.461 --> 00:05:36.482
These findings suggest a promising direction for further exploration.

89
00:05:36.907 --> 00:05:40.516
Understanding the design process can help a model judge quality,

90
00:05:41.044 --> 00:05:44.317
while learning to compare designs can also guide construction.

91
00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:49.162
At test time, the Critic can select better candidate edits to further improve

92
00:05:49.175 --> 00:05:50.103
scene quality.

93
00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:55.394
From bedrooms and living rooms to offices and other indoor spaces,

94
00:05:55.871 --> 00:05:59.299
our focus goes beyond where individual objects are placed.

95
00:05:59.724 --> 00:06:02.301
We care about how those objects form a complete,

96
00:06:02.598 --> 00:06:04.518
coherent, and usable space.

97
00:06:06.086 --> 00:06:09.901
We want AI to learn more than how to execute a single placement.

98
00:06:10.313 --> 00:06:12.839
We want it to understand why a design works,

99
00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.254
and use that understanding to make a better next decision.

100
00:06:16.666 --> 00:06:21.589
To design well, and understand why it is better. That is the design intelligence

101
00:06:21.602 --> 00:06:22.839
we aim to explore.
